---
/**
 * The launch screen: what fills the phone between tapping the icon and the block being on
 * screen, and the only part of the app that is allowed to be pure brand.
 *
 * It exists because a prerendered shell paints *instantly* and then sits there empty. The
 * skeletons underneath are the right answer for a card whose data is late, but they are
 * the wrong answer for the first half-second of a cold start: on a home-screen launch the
 * whole viewport is grey rectangles, which reads as a page that failed rather than as an
 * app opening. A native app covers that gap with its own mark, and so does this.
 *
 * Server-rendered, so it is in the first paint with no JavaScript behind it — an overlay
 * a script has to *insert* arrives after the empty screen it was meant to hide, which is
 * the one thing a splash must never do.
 *
 * The composition is `/login`'s header with the goal number taken out: the same tile, the
 * same mark, the same eyebrow. Those are the two screens without a dock, and someone
 * arriving at the app for the first time should see one shape, not two.
 *
 * The mark draws itself — five strokes staggered 60ms apart on `chart-draw`, the same
 * utility the fitness curve is drawn with, then the head lands. It is the app's one
 * decorative animation, and it is over in ~560ms, which is roughly what `/api/data` costs
 * on a phone. Under `prefers-reduced-motion` the whole thing collapses to a mark that is
 * simply *there* (see the blanket rule in `global.css`), which is why every animation here
 * ends on its resting state.
 *
 * ---
 *
 * **Three things keep it from becoming a bug.**
 *
 * 1. *It never outlives the data.* `src/lib/boot.ts` clears it the moment `useBlock`'s
 *    first `/api/data` settles — success or failure, because a fetch that came back wrong
 *    is a screen with an error card on it, not a screen still loading. That module also
 *    holds it for a 480ms floor: data cached at the edge can land in 90ms, and a mark that
 *    flashes half-drawn and vanishes is worse than no mark at all.
 * 2. *It cannot trap the app.* The script below is a dead-man switch — 2.6s from the page
 *    opening, whatever else happened, the overlay goes. A hung fetch then leaves the
 *    skeletons visible, which is a screen you can at least read. `<noscript>` removes it
 *    outright.
 * 3. *It is shown once per document, not once per tab.* `ClientRouter` swaps the whole
 *    body, so the incoming page's copy of this overlay would flash on every tab tap.
 *    `transition:persist` is exactly the escape hatch for that: the already-dismissed node
 *    is carried across the swap and the incoming one is discarded. It is left in the DOM
 *    hidden rather than removed, because persist can only keep a node that is still there.
 *
 * `role="status"` and not `role="alert"`: it is polite, and it is announced once.
 */
import { APP_NAME } from '../lib/config'
---

<div
  id="boot"
  transition:persist="boot"
  role="status"
  aria-label="Abriendo el bloque"
  class="fixed inset-0 z-50 grid place-items-center bg-surface px-gutter"
>
  <div class="flex flex-col items-center">
    {/*
      The same tile `/login` opens with. The mark is inlined rather than `<img>`ed here
      because the strokes are what animates, and a document cannot reach inside an image.
      It is `favicon.svg`'s own geometry — edit one and the other has to follow.
    */}
    <span
      class="performance-shadow grid size-16 place-items-center rounded-2xl border border-line bg-surface-raised"
    >
      <svg
        aria-hidden="true"
        viewBox="0 0 512 512"
        class="size-10 text-lime"
        fill="none"
        stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="38"
        stroke-linecap="round"
        stroke-linejoin="round"
      >
        {/*
          `pathLength="1"` on every stroke is what lets one keyframe draw all five: the
          dash arithmetic is normalised, so a 90-unit torso and a 190-unit leg take the
          same time and land together. Without it each path would need its real length
          measured in JavaScript.
        */}
        <path class="chart-draw" pathLength="1" d="M277 179 230 269" />
        <path class="chart-draw" pathLength="1" style="animation-delay: 60ms" d="m269 191-70 25-48-46" />
        <path class="chart-draw" pathLength="1" style="animation-delay: 120ms" d="m270 190 76 24 48-42" />
        <path class="chart-draw" pathLength="1" style="animation-delay: 180ms" d="m230 269-65 69-70 37" />
        <path class="chart-draw" pathLength="1" style="animation-delay: 240ms" d="m230 269 82 46 105 65" />
        {/* The head is a fill, not a stroke, so it arrives rather than draws. */}
        <circle class="fade-in" style="animation-delay: 300ms" cx="314" cy="112" r="34" fill="currentColor" stroke="none" />
      </svg>
    </span>

    <p class="mt-4 text-caption2 font-semibold uppercase tracking-[0.16em] text-label-3">
      {APP_NAME}
    </p>

    {/*
      The one thing on screen that keeps moving after the mark has settled, so a slow
      morning on 3G reads as *loading* rather than as stuck. Mint, because this is the app
      telling you it is working — the same reason the primary button is accent.

      Indeterminate on purpose: the only progress this could report is one fetch, and a bar
      that fakes a percentage of a request is a bar that lies. It is `ProgressBar`'s track
      geometry (`h-1.5`, `bg-fill-strong`) with a segment travelling through it.
    */}
    <span aria-hidden="true" class="mt-3.5 block h-1.5 w-20 overflow-hidden rounded-full bg-fill-strong">
      <span class="boot-rail block h-full w-2/5 rounded-full bg-accent"></span>
    </span>
  </div>
</div>

<noscript>
  <style>
    /* Nothing will ever dismiss it, and every screen behind it needs a script anyway. */
    #boot {
      display: none;
    }
  </style>
</noscript>

<style>
  /**
   * The exit, and the rail's travel. Both live here rather than in `global.css` because
   * both are single-use: this is the only overlay in the app and the only indeterminate
   * indicator in it.
   *
   * The dismissal is a *transition*, not an animation, so it runs off an attribute the
   * data layer sets and needs no second class to be cleaned up. `visibility` is on a
   * delayed 0s step so it flips only once the fade has finished — `display` cannot be
   * transitioned, and leaving the node merely transparent would keep it swallowing every
   * tap on the screen underneath.
   *
   * Both the fade and the rail collapse correctly under `prefers-reduced-motion`: the
   * blanket rule in `global.css` cuts the transition to nothing (the overlay simply goes)
   * and the rail's animation with it, which leaves the segment where its own CSS puts it
   * — parked at the left of the track, a static indicator rather than an empty groove.
   * That is why the travel is an animation over `translate3d` and not a `both`-filled one.
   */
  #boot {
    transition:
      opacity var(--duration-standard) var(--ease-ios),
      visibility 0s linear var(--duration-standard);

    /* The page's own ground rather than a flat fill. `body` paints a radial highlight from
       above the fold and pins it to the viewport with `background-attachment: fixed` —
       which is exactly this overlay's box, since it is a fixed child of `body`. Inheriting
       both makes the splash and the screen it uncovers the same surface, so the fade has
       nothing to cross but the mark. */
    background-image: inherit;
    background-attachment: fixed;
  }

  #boot[data-done] {
    opacity: 0;
    visibility: hidden;
  }

  @keyframes boot-rail {
    0% {
      transform: translate3d(-100%, 0, 0);
    }
    100% {
      transform: translate3d(250%, 0, 0);
    }
  }

  .boot-rail {
    animation: boot-rail 1.4s var(--ease-ios) infinite;
  }
</style>

<script>
  /**
   * The dead-man switch. `src/lib/boot.ts` is what normally clears the overlay, on the
   * first `/api/data`; this is the guarantee that a fetch which never settles — a dropped
   * connection on a phone that has just come out of a tunnel — cannot leave the app behind
   * a screen with no way past it.
   *
   * Measured from the page opening rather than from this script running, so a slow bundle
   * spends the budget instead of extending it.
   */
  const CEILING_MS = 2600

  setTimeout(
    () => document.getElementById('boot')?.setAttribute('data-done', ''),
    Math.max(0, CEILING_MS - performance.now()),
  )
</script>
